3599M", "latest_revision": 1, "revision": 1, "created": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2025-01-25T05:06:27.976373"}, "last_modified": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2025-01-25T05:06:27.976373"}}}, "OLID:OL35088241M": {"bib_key": "OLID:OL35088241M", "info_url": "https://openlibrary.org/books/OL35088241M/Born_to_Run", "preview": "restricted", "preview_url": "https://archive.org/details/borntorunhiddent0000mcdo", "thumbnail_url": "https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/12063992-S.jpg", "details": {"description": {"type": "/type/text", "value": "An epic adventure that began with one simple question: Why does my foot hurt?\r\n\r\nIsolated by Mexico's deadly Copper Canyons, the blissful Tarahumara Indi- ans have honed the ability to run hundreds of miles without rest or injury.\r\n\r\nIn a riveting narrative, award-winning journalist and often-injured runner Christopher McDougall sets out to discover their secrets. In the process, he takes his readers from science labs at Harvard to the sun- baked valleys and freezing peaks across North America, where ever- growing numbers of ultra-runners are pushing their bodies to the limit, and, finally, to a climactic race in the Copper Canyons that pits America's best ultra-runners against the tribe. McDougall's incredible story will not only engage your mind but inspire your body when you realize that you, indeed all of us, were born to run.\r\n--back cover"}, "notes": {"type": "/type/text", "value": "US/CAN"}, "identifiers": {"goodreads": ["59250955"]}, "title": "Born to Run", "publish_date": "2011-03", "publishers": ["Vintage Books"], "type": {"key": "/type/edition"}, "publish_places": ["New York"], "languages": [{"key": "/languages/eng"}], "copyright_date": "2009", "edition_name": "First Vintage Books Edition (10)", "pagination": "287p.", "physical_format": "Paperback", "covers": [12063992], "ocaid": "borntorunhiddent0000mcdo", "isbn_10": ["0307279189"], "isbn_13": ["9780307279187"], "lccn": ["2009922861"], "oclc_number